   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the agriculture and markets law, in relation to eligibility\nfor the appropriation of moneys for the promotion of agriculture and domestic\narts",
    "basePrintNo": "S5526",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5526-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §286, Ag & Mkts L",
    "lawSection": "Agriculture and Markets Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S5526\n\nSPONSOR: HINCHEY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the agriculture and markets law, in relation to eligi-\nbility for the appropriation of moneys for the promotion of agriculture\nand domestic arts\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo allow smaller fairs to be eligible for reimbursement of premiums\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends section 286 of the agriculture and markets law to\nlower the required amount of premiums to be paid to be eligible for\nreimbursement. Section 2 two establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe appropriations of moneys for the promotion of agriculture and domes-\ntic arts are an important part of supporting youth involvement at county\nfairs throughout the state. These premiums are paid by the fairs as\nawards to youth in competitions such as arts and crafts, livestock, and\nshowmanship. Currently, fairs must pay at least $5,000 in premiums to\nbecome eligible for state reimbursement. In contrast, county extension\nservices need only pay $500 in premiums to be eligible. Some fairs, such\nas the Afton Fair, Cortland Fair, Caledonia Fair (Livingston County),\nand Grahamsville Little World's Fair, are smaller than others. Because\nthese fairs do not reach the $5,000 threshold for premiums paid, they do\nnot currently qualify for state reimbursement.To support all the enti-\nties that promote agriculture and domestic arts in the state, no matter\nwhat their size, the state must begin offering a $2500 reimbursement\nthreshold to county fairs, all of which organize these important compe-\ntitions for New York State youth.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2022 - A.9717 - Referred to Agriculture\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act will take effect immediately.\n",
